course: dictionary
lesson: 4780
title: Huffy: sulking when feelings are hurt
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Huffy is an everyday word for someone who is easily hurt and then sulks. When your feelings are hurt, you might go quiet and cross. «Don't get huffy» is what you might say to a friend who has taken offence.
explain: «You're very alike in many respects, particularly about the figure.» Toad is huffy here. He takes offence at the remark about his figure and answers back at once.
explain: Use the clues around the word. Who is huffy, and what happened just before? Toad gets huffy after someone speaks about his figure, so huffy must mean upset and touchy. Swap in your meaning to check that it fits.
fact: Huffy is an informal, everyday word.
fact: «Huffy» means easily offended, or sulky and annoyed because your feelings are hurt.
fact: The forms are huffy, huffier and huffiest.
fact: In Alice in Wonderland, many creatures are huffy at Alice's harmless words.
fact: A huffy character often goes off in a sulk.
exercise: «Don't get huffy.» What does huffy mean? | Covered in soft fluff | *Easily offended and sulky | Happy and singing | Very fast and noisy
exercise: Toad is huffy when she says, «So has my aunt, for what she is.» What has upset him? | A prize he won | *Being teased about his figure | Being given a cake | The rain
exercise: In Alice in Wonderland, the creatures are huffy at Alice's harmless words. What does harmless mean? | *Not hurtful | Very loud | Hard to see | Made of wood
test: In a story, a huffy character does what? | *Goes off in a sulk | Shouts for joy and dances | Hides the cake | Falls asleep at once
test: Which word is closest in meaning to huffy? | *Sulky | Brave | Cheerful | Quick
exam: Which sentence uses huffy correctly? | She was huffy, so she laughed loudly at the joke | The huffy apple was red and sweet | *He went off in a sulk after his friend teased him | The huffy sun was bright and warm
